# Template for .env, which is gitignored. Copy it before editing:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# You do not need a .env to run Growthmind. Outside production every variable
# in the first section falls back to the value shown here, and docker-compose.yml
# bakes the same ones in, so `docker compose up` from a clean clone reaches a
# working app with no file at all. Copy this one when you run outside compose
# (`bun run dev` against your own Postgres), when you deploy, or to override a
# default. docs/first-run-setup.md walks the same ground as steps rather than
# as variables, if that is the way round you would rather read it.
#
# Every variable is listed in one of two sections: required to boot, or
# optional. Nothing below the divider will ever stop the app starting. Each
# entry says what it is for and what happens when it is absent, so nothing here
# needs a trip into the source to understand. The schemas those answers come
# from are in packages/shared/src/env.ts.
#
# THERE IS ONE SCHEMA PER PROCESS, not one for the repository. The web server
# parses the web schema, the worker parses the worker schema, and migrations and
# the key scripts parse the base schema they share. A variable is required only
# of the process that reads it: the worker needs no BETTER_AUTH_* at all, and a
# deployment that sets them on the web service alone is correct rather than
# half-configured. Each entry below says which process reads it.
#
# Client-exposed variables must be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which inlines them
# into the JavaScript bundle. Anything secret must not carry that prefix.
# This file is committed and the repository is world-readable — placeholder
# values only, never real ones.

# Required to boot

# Four variables have no fallback in production, and WHICH PROCESS NEEDS WHICH
# is the whole point of the split:
#
#   Every process:  DATABASE_URL, GROWTHMIND_ENCRYPTION_KEY
#   The web server: BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, BETTER_AUTH_URL — and NOT the worker.
#
# With NODE_ENV=production and one of its own missing, that process refuses to
# start and names the one it wanted. Anywhere else they fall back to the values
# in this section. (NODE_ENV itself is not set here — the tooling sets it:
# development under `bun run dev`, production in the compose stack and in a real
# deployment.)
#
# The worker used to require the two BETTER_AUTH_ variables as well, and a
# deployment that set them on the web service alone crash-looped the worker at
# boot on a value it never read — every scheduled task stopped, and the only
# symptom was a restart count. Requiring a variable of a process that does not
# consume it buys nothing and can only ever fail a boot it was not protecting.
#
# One more variable sits in this section without being required:
# GROWTHMIND_ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS, because it is about the four above and
# belongs nowhere else. Read its note before you deploy.

# Postgres, with pgvector available as an extension. The compose stack creates
# this exact database; point it elsewhere if you run your own.
#
# Absent in production: refuses to start. Absent anywhere else: this value.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://growthmind:growthmind@localhost:5432/growthmind

# Signs session cookies. Generate a real one for anything reachable from the
# internet:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Absent in production: refuses to start. Left at the published value below in
# production: also refuses — by value, not by absence, because anyone reading
# this repository could otherwise forge a session cookie for any user in any
# organisation. Absent anywhere else: this value.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=dev-only-secret-change-me-32-chars!

# The address this app is actually reachable at. Sign-in links are built from
# it, and so is the Slack callback address further down this file
# (apps/web/lib/slack/oauth.ts derives it from here rather than from a request
# header, because a header is caller-controlled and a redirect address built
# from one is an open redirect).
#
# Absent in production: refuses to start. Left at the published value below in
# production: also refuses, by value — the same treatment BETTER_AUTH_SECRET
# gets, and for a related reason. It used to default to http://localhost:3000 in
# every environment, production included, and nothing complained: a deployment
# that boots fine, signs people in through links pointing at a machine that is
# not yours, and tells Slack to deliver authorisation codes there too. Absent
# anywhere else: this value.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Encryption at rest for the analytics credentials people attach to a project.
# Base64 of 32 random bytes:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Absent in production: refuses to start. Absent anywhere else: a published
# dev-only key is used, which is why a fresh clone needs nothing here.
#
# Left at that published key in production, boot still succeeds — and then a
# second check refuses it at the moment a credential is about to be stored, so
# what stops is attaching an analytics account rather than the app. That check
# cannot be waived by GROWTHMIND_ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS below, because this key
# protects someone else's PostHog account rather than this deployment's own
# sessions.
# GROWTHMIND_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Not required, and not part of the validated schema. Set to 1 it permits the
# published example values above under NODE_ENV=production, which is why the
# quickstart compose stack sets it and still demos on a clean clone. Absent,
# those values are refused in production and the process says which one it
# found. Delete it from any real deployment. It does not waive the
# credential-key check described immediately above.
# GROWTHMIND_ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS=1

# Read by docker-compose.yml only, never by the app: it is the password compose
# gives Postgres and then builds DATABASE_URL from, for both containers. Absent,
# compose uses `growthmind`, which is safe only because the database port is
# bound to loopback. Running outside compose this does nothing — set the
# password inside DATABASE_URL above instead.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
#
# Nothing past this line is required, in any environment. Every one of these is
# a capability the app does without: absent, the feature it belongs to is not
# offered, and the app says so where a person would look for it. No variable
# below can stop the process starting.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Slack, the one-click path
#
# These two are the credentials of a Slack app YOU create. Together they put an
# "Add to Slack" button on the first-run screen: the person setting Growthmind
# up presses it, approves on Slack's own screen, and picks a channel from a live
# list. No token is typed and no channel ID is looked up.
#
# THE PRODUCT RUNS WITHOUT THEM, and not as a degraded mode. With neither set,
# the first-run screen's Slack step is the pasted-bot-token form, primary and
# unfolded — someone brings a bot token and a channel ID from a Slack app of
# their own, and setup completes exactly as it does on the one-click path.
# Self-host is first-class here (AGENTS.md, Conventions), so a clean clone with
# no Slack app must sign up, boot and finish setup identically to one that has
# a Slack app. Absent Slack credentials are never an error and never render as
# broken.
#
# BOTH OR NEITHER. One alone is the worst of the three states: the button
# renders, the person reaches Slack's consent screen, approves — and the token
# exchange dies, after they have already left the product. If you set one, set
# the other.
#
# Making the Slack app, click by click:
#
#   1. Open https://api.slack.com/apps and press "Create New App", then
#      "From scratch". Name it whatever the workspace should see (Growthmind is
#      fine) and pick the workspace you develop it in.
#   2. "OAuth & Permissions" in the sidebar, then "Redirect URLs" → "Add New
#      Redirect URL". Paste your callback address, then "Save URLs". The address
#      is BETTER_AUTH_URL with /api/first-run/slack/oauth/callback on the end —
#      so BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://growthmind.example.com makes it
#      https://growthmind.example.com/api/first-run/slack/oauth/callback.
#      Slack requires https and documents no exception for localhost, so a plain
#      http://localhost:3000 install cannot register one at all. Either run
#      behind an https tunnel and point BETTER_AUTH_URL (and ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS
#      below) at the tunnel address, or leave these two variables unset and use
#      the pasted bot token, which needs no redirect address of any kind.
#   3. Same page, "Scopes" → "Bot Token Scopes" → "Add an OAuth Scope", three
#      times. The three are channels:read, groups:read and chat:write, and they
#      are exactly SLACK_OAUTH_SCOPES in apps/web/lib/slack/channels.ts.
#      channels:read lists public channels, groups:read lists the private ones
#      the bot has been invited to, and chat:write is the one not to leave out:
#      scopes are granted when the workspace approves, so a workspace connected
#      without it lists channels, finishes setup, and can then never post — with
#      no error on any screen the person who set it up will ever see.
#   4. "Basic Information" → "App Credentials". Copy "Client ID" and
#      "Client Secret" into the two lines below.
#
# You do not install the app into a workspace yourself. Pressing "Add to Slack"
# on the first-run screen is what installs it, into whichever workspace the
# person approves it for.
#
# These are read by the web server process. Under `docker compose up` they need
# adding to the `web` service's `environment` in docker-compose.yml as well —
# compose passes into a container only what that file lists, so a value sitting
# in .env reaches the interpolation and not the app. Running `bun run dev`, .env
# is read directly and there is nothing else to do.
#
# Server-only, and there is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_ twin. Read from the
# browser the value is undefined, so the button would hide itself from exactly
# the deployments that configured a Slack app.
# SLACK_CLIENT_ID=
# SLACK_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Slack interactivity — the "Get it fixed" button on a delivered finding
#
# "Basic Information" → "App Credentials" → "Signing Secret" on the same Slack
# app as the pair above. Slack signs every button press with it, and the web
# server checks that signature before it does anything at all. Without it a
# press is refused in plain English and nothing is queued — the rest of the
# product, including delivery to Slack, is untouched.
#
# It also needs "Interactivity & Shortcuts" turned on in that same app, with the
# Request URL set to BETTER_AUTH_URL with /api/slack/interactivity on the end.
# Slack will only accept an https address there, so the same tunnel caveat as
# the redirect URL above applies.
#
# Read by the web server process only — the worker never answers a press, and a
# variable required of a process that does not read it can only ever fail a boot
# it was not protecting. Under `docker compose up` it is forwarded valueless on
# the `web` service, the same shape and for the same reason as the pair above.
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# Social sign-in — Google and GitHub
#
# Read by the web server. Each provider is BOTH OR NEITHER, for the same reason
# the Slack pair above is: one half alone renders a "Continue with Google"
# button, sends someone to Google's consent screen, and then fails the exchange
# after they have already left the product. Set both halves of a provider or
# neither; the two providers are independent of each other.
#
# THE PRODUCT RUNS WITHOUT THEM. With neither configured, the sign-in and
# sign-up screens show the email-and-password form alone — no disabled buttons,
# no divider with a gap above it, nothing to explain. Configure one and its
# button appears above the form on both screens.
#
# Server-only, and there is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_ twin: the browser is
# told WHICH providers are available, never the credentials. A test asserts no
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_*/NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_* name appears anywhere in the web
# sources.
#
# Redirect/callback URL to register with each provider is BETTER_AUTH_URL with
# /api/auth/callback/<provider> on the end — so BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://app.example.com
# makes it https://app.example.com/api/auth/callback/google.
#
#   Google:  https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → "Create
#            credentials" → "OAuth client ID" → "Web application". Add the
#            callback above under "Authorised redirect URIs".
#   GitHub:  https://github.com/settings/developers → "New OAuth App". The
#            callback above goes in "Authorization callback URL".
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Slack, the coming-soon interest note
#
# An incoming-webhook address in YOUR OWN Slack workspace — the operator's, not
# a customer's. The first-run screen lists connections that are not built yet,
# each with a "Ping me when it's ready" tap. When a workspace registers
# interest that way, a short note with the provider name and a running count is
# posted to this webhook, so demand for a connection is heard rather than
# guessed at. It has nothing to do with the two credentials above, which belong
# to the Slack app your customers approve.
#
# The product runs without it. Absent, the coming-soon chips read "Coming
# soon" with no button — there is no tap to take, so no interest is recorded
# and nothing is posted. Set, each chip gains the one-tap "Ping me when it's
# ready" button, the tap is recorded, and the note is posted to this channel.
# The value must be a well-formed address: a typo is refused at boot, in
# front of the operator who can fix it, rather than failing quietly on every
# later send. Server-only, and there is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_ twin —
# the browser learns only whether the note is configured, never the address.
#
# READ BY BOTH PROCESSES, and it must be set on both: the worker's minutely tick
# does the posting, and the web server reads the same value to answer "is the
# note configured" on the first-run screen. Set on the worker alone, the chips
# render with no button; set on the web server alone, the button is offered and
# nothing is ever posted.
# INTEREST_SLACK_WEBHOOK=

# Better Auth Infrastructure (dash.better-auth.com) API key. An operator
# convenience for this deployment, never a dependency of the product. Absent —
# the clean-clone and `docker compose up` case — the dash plugin is simply not
# registered (apps/web/lib/auth.ts) and auth behaves identically.
# BETTER_AUTH_API_KEY=

# Dev only. Comma-separated hosts allowed to reach the dev server's own
# resources (/_next/*, HMR) cross-origin — needed when you browse the app
# through a tunnel (ngrok etc.), which is also what the Slack redirect address
# above needs on a local machine. Schemes are tolerated and stripped; unset
# keeps the same-origin default. Production builds ignore this entirely.
# ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS=localhost:3000,https://your-tunnel.ngrok-free.dev

# Model key — optional, and always yours (product decisions §12: token spend
# is visible and on your budget). An Amazon Bedrock API key, minted in the
# Bedrock console; the model it talks to is Claude, see
# `GROWTHMIND_COLDSTART_MODEL` below.
#
# `AWS_REGION` below is part of the same switch, not a separate setting. A
# Bedrock API key is scoped to the region it was minted for, and the key
# authenticates with a bearer token rather than AWS credentials — so no
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID or IAM role is involved. Set both or neither: one alone
# cannot call anything, and the worker treats it as no model configured.
#
# Two lanes read it. Reading your own website to work out who your product is
# for ("who your product is for", on the growth-context page) needs it TODAY —
# unset, that page tells you no model is configured and reads nothing. The
# cold-start analysis lane also reads it and is wired to act on it, but has no
# production source of candidates on this branch yet (the assembler that turns
# sessions and events into gate-passed candidates is still to come), so for
# that lane alone, setting or unsetting this key changes nothing observable
# today (`worker/src/index.ts` carries the same admission at the composition
# root, and is the fresher word if the two ever disagree). Once that lane is
# wired, this is the switch it was always meant to be: leave it unset and
# every finding still ships, carrying its numbers with no written explanation
# attached; set it and the lane asks a model for that explanation, within a
# hard per-project limit.
#
# Read by the worker process. docker-compose.yml already forwards both to the
# worker container when the host or .env has them.
# AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK=
# AWS_REGION=eu-west-2

# Which model the two lanes above ask. Optional, and validated only if you set
# it. Leave it unset and both use the default,
# `eu.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0`
# (`packages/adapters/src/model/constants.ts`). That id names a EUROPEAN
# cross-region inference profile: set `AWS_REGION` outside the EU group and you
# must set this too, to that group's profile for the same model (`us.` for US
# regions, and so on). A model id from the wrong region group fails at call
# time, not at boot. The findings themselves never depend on it — only the
# plain-English explanation attached to them, which is dropped rather than
# guessed at whenever the model is unavailable, over budget, or returns
# something that does not pass the lane's accuracy check.
#
# The hard per-project limit mentioned above is a constant, not an env var —
# a deliberate choice, not an oversight: `COLDSTART_MODEL_CALL_CAP` in
# `worker/src/analysis-cap.ts`. Change the number there if you want a
# different ceiling; there is no environment variable for it.
# GROWTHMIND_COLDSTART_MODEL=

# There are three unrelated PostHog integrations in this repo. Read this once
# and the two blocks below stop looking like duplicates of each other:
#
#   1. A CUSTOMER's PostHog — their credentials, encrypted per project in the
#      database. No environment variable, ever. A global key would make a
#      multi-tenant product single-tenant. This is the key someone pastes into
#      the first-run screen, and it goes nowhere near this file.
#   2. GROWTHMIND'S OWN product analytics — the NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_* block
#      immediately below. Unset means the app sends nothing anywhere.
#   3. The SPIKE HARNESSES under scripts/spikes — the POSTHOG_* block after
#      that. Local tooling only; the app never reads them.
#
# Nothing is shared between them. In particular (2) and (3) both want a phc_
# project key, and they should be DIFFERENT projects: the spike harnesses write
# synthetic events into theirs.

# Growthmind's own product analytics — where THIS app reports its own usage
# (sign-ups, sign-ins, client exceptions). Absent: the SDK never initialises,
# nothing is sent, and the app runs normally. That is the supported default for
# a self-hosted install.
#
# Works with a fully self-hosted PostHog. Set NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST to your
# own instance and every origin follows it — no request reaches posthog.com.
# On PostHog Cloud, set the api host and the asset/UI origins are derived from
# it. Resolution and its tests: apps/web/lib/posthog-hosts.ts.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN — project API key (phc_...) of the project
#   you want Growthmind's OWN events in. Unset disables analytics entirely.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST — the PostHog origin events are sent to.
#   Self-hosted: https://posthog.your-company.com
#   Cloud EU: https://eu.i.posthog.com (the default) · Cloud US: https://us.i.posthog.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_ASSETS_HOST — optional override for the origin serving
#   PostHog's static assets. Defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST, which is
#   correct for self-hosted; Cloud's split assets origin is derived for you.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_UI_HOST — optional override for links back into PostHog's
#   own UI. Same defaulting rule as the assets host.
#
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is required, not stylistic: Next.js only inlines
# variables with that prefix into the browser bundle, and it does so at BUILD
# time — change one of these and you must rebuild for it to take effect.
# Under compose that means `docker compose up --build`, and on a deploy target
# they must be set as BUILD variables: these four reach the image through
# `build.args`, never through the runtime environment.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=phc_your_project_api_key
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_ASSETS_HOST=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_UI_HOST=

# Session-replay capture — a fourth thing this block exists to stop you reading as a
# PostHog duplicate. Unrelated integration, unrelated vendor: rrweb.com, not PostHog.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RRWEB_PUBLIC_KEY is the CAPTURE-SIDE public key from your project at
# app.rrweb.com. It is safe to expose in the browser — it can only start a recording,
# not read one back. Reading recordings needs a separate read-scoped key, which this
# repo does not consume anywhere yet.
#
# Absent: apps/web/lib/rrweb-capture.ts never starts the recorder, and replay capture
# is off — the supported self-host default, same as the PostHog block above.
#
# BUILD-TIME only, same constraint and same reason as the four NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_*
# variables just above: Next.js inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* when it compiles, so this reaches
# the image only through docker-compose.yml's `web` service `build.args`, never through
# a runtime environment variable. Under compose that means `docker compose up --build`
# after setting it; on a deploy target it must be set as a BUILD variable.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT ON A SITE REAL PEOPLE USE. The recorder masks every text
# node and every input value, and does not mask HTML attributes — rrweb 2.1.1 serialises
# `title`, `alt`, `aria-label`, `placeholder` and `data-*` verbatim, with no option to
# mask them. A line as ordinary as `<td title={customer.fullName}>` therefore leaves the
# browser in the clear. No such binding exists in this app today, and a test
# (apps/web/__tests__/replay-attribute-exposure.test.ts) fails if one is added — but that
# test only reads this repo. If you have forked or added pages, check yours.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RRWEB_PUBLIC_KEY=

# PostHog, for local tooling only. These four are validated if you set them (so
# a typo fails at startup rather than halfway through a script), but THE APP
# STILL NEVER READS THEM: a customer's PostHog credentials are stored per
# project, encrypted, and read from the database. A global key here would be a
# single-tenant design in a multi-tenant product, so it does not exist.
#
# What they are actually for: `bun scripts/spikes/*`. Leaving all four unset is
# fully supported everywhere, including production — a self-hosted install with
# no PostHog boots normally and says plainly what is unavailable.
#
# Point them at a throwaway TEST PostHog project. The spike harnesses write
# synthetic events into it.
#
# POSTHOG_HOST — your PostHog region URL, e.g. https://us.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY — project API key (phc_...) from project settings;
#   used to capture (write) events. Not a secret, but use the test project's.
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY — personal API key (phx_...); a real secret. Used
#   to READ events/recordings; create it on PostHog's personal API keys page
#   with read scope for the test project.
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID — numeric project id from project settings.
# CHROME_PATH — optional; absolute path to a Chrome/Edge/Chromium binary for
#   the session-recording leg's headless browser. Auto-detected if unset.
# POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY=phc_your_project_api_key
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=phx_your_personal_api_key
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID=12345
# CHROME_PATH=

# Who may see the unfinished preview surfaces. Comma-separated; matches a user id OR an email.
# Absent means nobody sees them, which is the safe default.
GROWTHMIND_PREVIEW_USER_IDS=
